 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Differentiable Volumetric Rendering (DVR)](775-differentiable-volumetric-rendering-dvr.md)
-Predicts pixel color by casting a ray from a camera origin through a pixel, finding the first intersection with an implicit isosurface defined by a neural network, and querying a texture network at that point. It solves the problem of optimizing implicit neural representations of 3D shape and texture directly from 2D RGB images without requiring 3D ground truth supervision. Unlike voxel-based differentiable rendering methods or LSTM-based renderers, DVR does not store intermediate volumetric data or unrolled computation graphs for the forward pass, making its memory footprint independent of sampling accuracy and allowing for higher resolution.
+Differentiable Volumetric Rendering (DVR) is a method for training implicit neural representations of 3D shape and texture using only 2D RGB image supervision, eliminating the need for 3D ground truth data. It differs from voxel-based differentiable renderers by avoiding the storage of intermediate volumetric data, thereby achieving a memory footprint independent of sampling resolution. Unlike mesh-based methods, it represents geometry continuously as an isosurface of a neural network, allowing for arbitrary resolution extraction.
 
 ### [Differentiable Direct Volume Rendering (DiffDVR)](1355-differentiable-direct-volume-rendering-diffdvr.md)
-DiffDVR is an image-based optimization method that uses differentiable direct volume rendering to optimize transfer functions and other continuous parameters by minimizing residuals between rendered images and a reference. It sets itself apart from standard reverse-mode automatic differentiation by using analytic inversion of the blending functions to recompute intermediate results, thereby reducing memory consumption from $O(kn)$ to $O(n)$ without storing the computational graph. Unlike least-squares voxel-based solvers, DiffDVR accounts for ray-based integration effects like occlusion and attenuation, emphasizing structural differences in residual maps.
+DiffDVR is an image-based optimization method that uses differentiable direct volume rendering to optimize transfer functions and other continuous parameters by minimizing residuals between rendered images and a reference. It sets itself apart from standard reverse-mode automatic differentiation by using analytic inversion of the blending functions to recompute intermediate results, thereby reducing memory consumption from $O(kn)$ to $O(n)$ without storing the computational graph. Unlike least-squares voxel-based solvers, DiffDVR accounts for ray-based integration effects like occlusion and attenuation, emphasizing structural differences in residual maps. It builds on the emission-absorption model shared by [Differentiable Direct Volume Rendering (DVR)](8871-differentiable-direct-volume-rendering-dvr.md) and differs from [Differentiable Volumetric Rendering (DVR)](775-differentiable-volumetric-rendering-dvr.md) and [Differentiable DRR Generation via Vectorized GPU Siddon's Method](9914-differentiable-drr-generation-via-vectorized-gpu-siddon-s-me.md) in its explicit analytic inversion of light accumulation along view rays.
 
