+#### Generalized Direct Volume Rendering
+- **Definition**: A differentiable formulation of DVR that generalizes the emission-absorption model to arbitrary feature spaces and color channels, allowing replaceable functions for feature extraction, TFs, composition, and decoding [S1, unofficial].
+- **Step**: The pipeline consists of three parts: (1) Feature extraction $E(\mathbf{I})$, (2) Deep volume rendering (sampling and accumulation), (3) Image decoding $D(\mathbf{C})$ [S1, unofficial].
+- **Formula**: Feature extraction transforms scalar input volume $\mathbf{I}$ to $n_F$-dimensional feature space: $\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}) = E(\mathbf{I}(\mathbf{x}))$ [S1, unofficial]. Samples $(\mathbf{C}_i, \mathbf{A}_i)$ are combined with an accumulation function $\mathcal{A}$ to a final ray color: $\mathbf{C}'_\infty = \mathcal{A}(\{(\mathbf{C}_i, \mathbf{A}_i)|i\})$ [S1, unofficial]. The image decoder transforms rendered features back to RGB: $\mathbf{C}^{RGB} = D(\mathbf{C}'_\infty)$ [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of differentiable volume rendering has evolved to address the limitations of standard rendering in optimization tasks. The foundational work on Differentiable Volumetric Rendering (DVR) for implicit neural representations was introduced by [S2][S3, unofficial]. The generalized formulation, which allows for learnable transfer functions and feature spaces, was proposed in [S1, unofficial]. DiffDVR was introduced to provide memory-efficient optimization of transfer functions [S2].
+The concept of differentiable volume rendering has evolved to support inverse rendering and neural implicit representations. Early work focused on optimizing transfer functions for visualization [S2]. More recently, methods like [Differentiable Volumetric Rendering (DVR)](775-differentiable-volumetric-rendering-dvr.md) have enabled the training of neural networks for 3D reconstruction from 2D images [S1, S2]. The generalized formulation allows for flexible integration of deep learning components into the rendering pipeline [S1, unofficial].
 
